===============================================================================
NEW ADDENDUM: GLOBAL MODULE ENABLE / DISABLE MANAGEMENT
ITEM MASTER AND BUSINESS PARTNER MASTER
===============================================================================

PURPOSE
-------
Implement a centrally managed, administrator-controlled Enable/Disable option
for each primary business module in the Kosh Innovations Global IT System.

Initial primary modules:
1. Item Master
2. Business Partner Master (BP Master)

The module control is a functional feature flag and access-control gate. It must
not be implemented as visual menu hiding alone. Module state must consistently
control navigation, page access, actions, APIs, background jobs, scheduled
processing, imports/exports, approvals, and integrations.

IMPORTANT PROJECT PRESERVATION RULE
-----------------------------------
Preserve all existing application features, pages, field definitions, business
rules, data, SAP mappings, integrations, workflows, layouts, and prior
requirements. Add this capability to the existing application. Do not rebuild
the app from scratch, remove existing functionality, or replace previous
requirements with a shortened implementation.

A. CENTRAL MODULE MANAGEMENT PAGE
--------------------------------
Create an administrator-only page named "Module Management" or
"Module Enable / Disable" under the existing Settings / General Settings area.
Use the current portal visual style and navigation structure.

Show one card or row for each primary module:
- Item Master
- Business Partner Master

Each module row/card must display:
- Module name and description
- Enabled / Disabled state
- Toggle or explicit Enable and Disable actions
- Last changed timestamp
- Changed by user
- Optional reason/comment for the change
- Dependency/shared-feature warning where relevant
- A clear indication of whether the module is currently available
- A confirmation step before disabling a module

Only users with a specifically authorized permission such as
MODULE_CONFIGURATION_MANAGE may change module states. A general administrator
label in the UI is not sufficient authorization by itself; enforce the
permission on the server.

Default state for this initial rollout:
- Item Master: ENABLED
- Business Partner Master: ENABLED

If existing deployments already have established module states, migrate them
safely and preserve those states rather than blindly resetting them. If there
is no prior configuration, initialize both modules as enabled so that existing
functionality is not unexpectedly hidden.

B. ITEM MASTER MODULE DEPENDENCY MAP
------------------------------------
When Item Master is ENABLED, show and allow access to all existing Item Master
features that the current user is otherwise authorized to use.

The Item Master module includes, at minimum, the following existing
Item Master-specific navigation entries and features. Inspect the actual
current project and include every related entry, including any additional
entries discovered in the codebase:

- Item
- Create Item / Item Creation Form
- Global Item Master
- Global Item Master search, sort, filter, pagination and list actions
- Item Master import, bulk import, export and templates
- My Requests when the request is an Item Master request
- Item Master approval queues and approval actions
- Item Master-specific Custom Specifications, including the
  Customer / Manufacturer / Brand (Master) and
  Customer / Part / Model Number specifications if they are currently
  implemented as Item Master configuration
- Item Code & Description
- Item Code & Description rule configuration
- Classification Masters used by Item Master
- Branch Item Availability
- Item Master-specific validation, duplicate detection and code allocation
- Item Master-specific governance, audit views and rule-version history
- Item Master SAP creation/synchronization, scheduling, safe testing,
  activation, monitor/recovery and retry functions where those functions
  process Item Master records
- Item Master-specific notifications, imports, exports, APIs and background
  jobs
- Dashboard cards, widgets, quick actions and counts that are exclusively
  based on Item Master
- Any other route, tab, dialog, action or setting whose function is specific
  to Item Master

When Item Master is DISABLED:
- Hide or disable the top-level Item navigation entry.
- Hide or disable all Item Master-specific submenus listed above.
- Disable Item Master-specific configuration sections, including Item Code &
  Description and Classification Masters used only by Item Master.
- Disable Item Master-specific Custom Specifications where they are part of
  Item Master configuration.
- Disable Item Master creation, editing, submission, approval, bulk import,
  export, and SAP synchronization actions.
- Prevent direct navigation to Item Master routes, even if a user pastes a URL.
- Reject Item Master-specific API requests on the server.
- Prevent Item Master-specific scheduled jobs and workers from starting new
  processing while the module is disabled.
- Prevent queued Item Master jobs from being picked up for new processing.
- Do not delete, archive, clear, or rewrite existing Item Master data.
- Do not silently alter already issued Item Codes, approved records, rules,
  audit entries, or SAP-created records.
- Keep shared infrastructure and shared menus enabled if another active module
  needs them.
- Do not disable BP Master merely because Item Master is disabled.

If a user tries to open an Item Master route directly while the module is
disabled, show a clear "Item Master is currently disabled" page or message,
with no data or mutation actions exposed. Do not render the disabled page
behind a transparent overlay as a substitute for access enforcement.

C. BUSINESS PARTNER MASTER DEPENDENCY MAP
-----------------------------------------
When Business Partner Master is ENABLED, show and allow access to all existing
BP Master features that the current user is otherwise authorized to use.

The BP Master module includes, at minimum, the following features. Inspect the
actual current project and include all related entries found in the codebase:

- Business Partner Master navigation entry
- Business Partner creation and editing forms
- Business Partner list, search, sort, filter and pagination
- Customer, Supplier/Vendor, Lead and other supported BP types
- General data, contacts, addresses, payment/accounting fields and supported
  properties
- BP Master-specific Custom Specifications and field configuration
- BP Master import, bulk import, export and templates
- BP Master validation, duplicate detection and approval workflows
- GSTIN/PAN validation and GST verification workflow
- BP Master audit history and governance
- BP Master SAP Business One HANA creation, update, synchronization, scheduling,
  monitoring, recovery and retry functions
- BP Master-specific notifications, APIs, queues and background jobs
- BP Master-specific dashboard cards, widgets and quick actions
- Any additional BP-specific route, tab, dialog, action or configuration page

When Business Partner Master is DISABLED:
- Hide or disable the BP Master top-level navigation entry and its submenus.
- Disable BP creation, editing, submission, approval, import, export and
  SAP synchronization actions.
- Disable BP-specific Custom Specifications and field configuration.
- Disable BP-specific GST verification initiation and processing. Existing
  verification history must remain available only to authorized audit or
  compliance users if governance policy permits read-only access.
- Prevent direct route access and reject BP-specific API requests server-side.
- Prevent new BP-specific background jobs from starting while disabled.
- Preserve all existing BP records, SAP identifiers, verification history,
  approvals and audit records.
- Do not disable Item Master or shared features still required by Item Master.

D. SHARED MENUS AND SHARED SERVICES
-----------------------------------
Do not assume every menu belongs exclusively to one module. Inspect the current
navigation and route tree and assign each menu item an explicit module
dependency.

Use one of these classifications for every navigation item, route, API and job:
1. ITEM_MASTER_ONLY
2. BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER_ONLY
3. SHARED
4. PLATFORM_ADMIN_ONLY
5. OTHER_MODULE_ONLY, if additional modules exist

Shared examples may include:
- Login, session management and security
- User and role administration
- Organization and branch management
- Global dashboard shell and layout
- Audit infrastructure
- Communication and notification infrastructure
- SAP connection profiles and credential vault references
- Shared monitoring and health checks
- Shared classification services, if genuinely used by both modules

The actual dependency must be determined from the existing implementation.
Do not classify a shared feature as exclusive without inspecting its consumers.

If both Item Master and BP Master use a shared configuration or SAP connection:
- Keep the shared page accessible to authorized administrators while at least
  one dependent module is enabled, unless the page is explicitly platform-wide.
- Keep independent connection health monitoring available to platform
  administrators even if a business module is disabled.
- Disable only the operations associated with the disabled module.
- Where a shared screen has separate Item and BP sections, disable only the
  section for the disabled module.
- Where a shared action cannot safely be separated, explain the dependency
  and require a deliberate administrator confirmation instead of silently
  breaking the active module.

Examples:
- Disabling Item Master must not disable BP Master GST verification if BP Master
  remains enabled.
- Disabling BP Master must not disable Item Code generation if Item Master
  remains enabled.
- Disabling either module must not disable login, role management, platform
  audit, or general SAP connection health checks.
- If SAP Integration Management contains both Item and BP jobs, keep the page
  visible to authorized administrators and independently disable only the job
  groups belonging to the disabled module.

E. MENU AND ROUTE IMPLEMENTATION
--------------------------------
Implement a central module registry and a single effective-state service.
Avoid scattered hardcoded booleans across unrelated components.

Suggested conceptual registry:
- module_key: ITEM_MASTER
- module_key: BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER
- display_name
- description
- is_enabled
- changed_at
- changed_by
- change_reason
- configuration_version

Each navigation item and route must declare its required module or modules.
A route with multiple dependencies is available only when its documented
dependency rule is satisfied. Prefer explicit dependency metadata rather than
matching route names or labels using fragile string comparisons.

On the client:
- Build navigation from the effective module state and the user's permissions.
- Remove disabled module menus from normal navigation or render them disabled
  with an explicit reason, according to the existing UI convention. The
  default behavior should be to hide the disabled module's entire navigation
  subtree, as requested.
- Ensure collapsed and expanded sidebar states behave consistently.
- Remove or disable related dashboard cards, quick links, tabs, buttons, and
  contextual actions.
- Do not leave blank menu groups, orphaned separators or dead links.
- Refresh effective module state after an authorized state change.
- Handle a stale browser tab gracefully if the module is disabled elsewhere.

On the server:
- Enforce module state on every protected API and mutation endpoint.
- Do not trust a client-side hidden menu as an authorization mechanism.
- Return a consistent structured error for a disabled module, such as HTTP 403
  with a stable error code MODULE_DISABLED and the module key.
- Do not leak protected record details in the error response.
- Ensure direct URL navigation cannot bypass the module gate.
- Ensure background workers and scheduled tasks use the same authoritative
  module-state service.

F. DISABLE CONFIRMATION AND OPERATIONAL SAFETY
----------------------------------------------
Before disabling a module, display a confirmation dialog that identifies:
- The module being disabled
- The menu and functions that will become unavailable
- The impact on new creation, updates, approvals, imports/exports and SAP sync
- The treatment of currently running jobs
- Any pending queue items that will be paused
- Shared pages that will remain available
- A required confirmation action
- An optional reason for the change

The system must not delete or cancel queued records merely because a module is
disabled.

When disabling a module:
1. Persist the new authoritative state transactionally.
2. Make the module unavailable for new operations.
3. Signal active workers to stop claiming new jobs for that module.
4. Let in-flight operations finish only when safe and policy-approved, or stop
   them at a safe checkpoint. Do not abruptly interrupt a transaction in a way
   that risks partial SAP or database writes.
5. Mark paused work with an explicit status such as PAUSED_MODULE_DISABLED.
6. Preserve retry counts, idempotency keys, job payload references and audit
   history.
7. Show the administrator the number of running and pending jobs, where that
   information is available.
8. Record the module-state change in the audit log.

When enabling a module:
1. Verify the user has permission.
2. Persist the enabled state.
3. Refresh menu, route and API effective state.
4. Allow eligible queued jobs to resume according to normal scheduling rules.
5. Revalidate jobs before resuming them; do not replay already completed work.
6. Do not automatically create or synchronize historical records unless an
   explicitly configured recovery policy requests it.
7. Audit the change and show the result.

If the module-state service or database is unavailable, fail closed for new
writes and integration actions for modules whose enabled state cannot be
verified. Do not assume that a module is enabled because the client cannot
load its configuration. Continue to provide a clear operational error and
platform-admin recovery route.

G. DATA MODEL AND CONFIGURATION STORAGE
---------------------------------------
Add an appropriate migration using the application's existing database and
migration conventions. Do not create duplicate configuration tables if an
equivalent reliable module-registry table already exists.

Suggested table: system_module_registry

Fields:
- id (primary key)
- module_key (unique, non-null)
- display_name
- description
- is_enabled (non-null boolean)
- configuration_version
- changed_at (timestamp with timezone)
- changed_by (user reference where supported)
- change_reason (nullable)
- created_at
- updated_at

Suggested module keys:
- ITEM_MASTER
- BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER

Use a separate immutable audit/event table or the existing audit infrastructure
to record:
- Event ID
- Module key
- Previous state
- New state
- User identity
- Timestamp
- Reason/comment
- Correlation/request ID
- Result
- Relevant job impact counts, if available

Use the existing audit implementation where suitable rather than introducing a
parallel audit system without justification.

Protect against concurrent administrators changing the same module at the
same time. Use transactions and optimistic concurrency/version checks or an
equivalent safe mechanism.

Do not allow ordinary module configuration to override security permissions,
data-governance controls, credential protection, or platform-wide safety rules.

H. ROLE-BASED AUTHORIZATION
---------------------------
Implement permissions such as:
- MODULE_CONFIGURATION_VIEW
- MODULE_CONFIGURATION_MANAGE
- ITEM_MASTER_ACCESS
- ITEM_MASTER_CREATE
- ITEM_MASTER_UPDATE
- ITEM_MASTER_APPROVE
- ITEM_MASTER_IMPORT
- ITEM_MASTER_EXPORT
- BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER_ACCESS
- BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER_CREATE
- BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER_UPDATE
- BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER_APPROVE
- BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER_IMPORT
- BUSINESS_PARTNER_MASTER_EXPORT
- BUSINESS_PARTNER_GST_VERIFY

Reuse equivalent existing permissions where present. Do not create duplicate
permission models without first inspecting the existing role system.

Effective access must require both:
1. The relevant module is enabled.
2. The current user has the required permission.

Enabling a module does not grant access to every user. Disabling a module does
not remove roles, permissions, user assignments or audit history.

I. DASHBOARD AND REPORTING BEHAVIOR
-----------------------------------
- Hide Item Master-only cards, charts, counts, shortcuts and recent-record
  sections when Item Master is disabled.
- Hide BP Master-only cards, charts, counts, shortcuts and recent-record
  sections when BP Master is disabled.
- If the dashboard includes a shared SAP integration card, retain it when
  needed by an enabled module or for platform-admin monitoring.
- Do not display fabricated zero counts when a section is hidden or unavailable.
- Label disabled, unavailable, paused and failed states accurately.
- Keep unrelated module dashboards operational.
- If both business modules are disabled, preserve the platform shell and
  authorized administrative recovery features.

J. ACCEPTANCE TESTS — REQUIRED
------------------------------
Implement and run tests; do not claim completion merely because the UI toggle
is visible.

Test 1 — Initial configuration:
- If no prior registry exists, Item Master and BP Master are both enabled.
- Existing menus and routes remain available to authorized users.

Test 2 — Disable Item Master:
- Item navigation disappears from the sidebar.
- Create Item, Global Item Master, Item-specific Custom Specifications,
  Item Code & Description, Classification Masters used only by Item Master,
  Branch Item Availability and all other Item-only submenus disappear.
- Direct Item Master URL access is blocked.
- Item Master API reads/writes are rejected according to the policy.
- Item Master creation, approval, imports, exports and SAP jobs cannot start.
- Existing data and audit history are preserved.
- BP Master, its forms, its GST verification and BP-specific SAP processing
  remain available if BP Master is enabled.
- Shared platform pages and services continue to work.

Test 3 — Re-enable Item Master:
- Item Master menus and authorized routes return.
- Existing data and rules are intact.
- No duplicate item records are created.
- Paused eligible jobs resume only under safe retry and idempotency rules.

Test 4 — Disable BP Master:
- BP Master navigation and all BP-only submenus disappear.
- Direct BP routes and BP APIs are blocked.
- BP creation, approvals, imports/exports, GST verification initiation and
  BP SAP jobs are disabled.
- Item Master and its code generation remain operational if enabled.

Test 5 — Re-enable BP Master:
- BP menus and authorized routes return.
- Existing BP records, GST verification history and SAP mappings remain intact.
- Queued work resumes safely without duplicating records.

Test 6 — Shared menus:
- SAP Integration Management remains accessible to authorized platform
  administrators.
- Item and BP job groups can be controlled independently.
- Disabling one module does not break shared services used by the other.

Test 7 — Authorization:
- A user without MODULE_CONFIGURATION_MANAGE cannot toggle module states,
  even by calling the API directly.
- A user without module-specific access cannot use the module merely because
  it is enabled.

Test 8 — Race conditions and stale sessions:
- Simultaneous toggle requests are handled safely.
- A browser tab with stale state cannot submit protected writes after disable.
- Workers do not claim new jobs after the disabled state is effective.

Test 9 — Failure and recovery:
- Database or state-service errors do not silently default to enabled.
- Failed disable/enable operations show an accurate result.
- Audit entries are created only with the actual committed state transition.

Test 10 — Regression:
- Existing Item Master, BP Master, SAP HANA integration, GST verification,
  import/export, governance, notifications and dashboard features still work
  when their modules are enabled.
- No prior requirements are removed or weakened.

K. IMPLEMENTATION PROCESS
-------------------------
1. Inspect the current project files, route definitions, sidebar/navigation
   components, page components, API routers, permission model, database models,
   migrations, scheduler, background workers, SAP integration jobs, dashboard
   widgets and tests.
2. Produce a dependency inventory mapping every route, menu, API, action,
   configuration section and background job to its module key.
3. Identify genuinely shared pages and functions.
4. Implement the database-backed module registry and safe migration.
5. Implement server-side module gating and permission checks.
6. Implement the Module Management page and confirmation workflow.
7. Apply effective module state to sidebar navigation, routes, dashboards,
   configuration pages, action buttons, imports/exports, approval queues,
   schedulers and workers.
8. Implement safe pause/resume handling for module-specific background work.
9. Run migrations, lint/type checks, unit tests, integration tests and the
   acceptance tests above using the actual available project environment.
10. Inspect the rendered UI for both enabled and disabled states.
11. Report files changed, migration details, routes/jobs gated, test commands,
    test outcomes and any limitations. Do not claim a test passed unless it
    was actually run successfully.

Do not stop after implementing the visual toggle. The server-side enforcement,
navigation dependencies, background-job behavior, persistence, audit logging
and regression tests are all part of the requirement.

FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
-------------------------
An authorized administrator can independently enable or disable Item Master
and BP Master from one central settings page. Each state persists across
sessions and application restarts. Disabling a module disables its entire
dependent feature set throughout the application while leaving the other
enabled module and genuinely shared platform services functional. Enabling a
module restores access without data loss, rule loss, permission escalation or
duplicate processing. All state changes are auditable and enforced by the
server, not merely by the client UI.

